 "title": "Ego Diversification: Surviving Corporate Humiliation",
 "topic": "mindset",
 "summary": "The creator suggests that corporate work diminishes self-respect and offers three steps of ego diversification to cope when quitting is not an option. The main lesson is to reframe workplace disrespect as spiritual growth and build competence outside of work.",
 "key_points": [
  "Rebrand workplace humiliation as spiritual growth (e.g., coworker ignoring ideas is detachment, someone taking credit is surrender).",
  "Brainwash yourself into believing your paycheck works for you, and they are paying for the privilege of your time.",
  "Get regular proof of competence outside of work where no one can rate you (run a half marathon, train for HYROX, become a parent of plants/pets/humans, or have strong opinions on coffee and tea)."
 ],
 "content": "If your self-respect is OK after working corporate, you don't need this. After working for some time in corporate, I realized that along with taxes, my self-respect was also being deducted with every paycheck. And as much as I'd love to live without it, turns out we all need some ego and self-respect to survive. No, quitting wasn't an option since my backup plan was basically waiting for a Nigerian prince to email me about my inheritance. So, I thought really hard about it and I came up with a more sensible solution. This solution is called ego diversification. And in my experience, I found there are three steps to do it. Number one, rebrand workplace humiliation as spiritual growth. When your colleague ignores your ideas, that's detachment. When someone takes credit for your work, that's surrender. Remember, you're not being disrespected. You're becoming enlightened through ego death. Two, brainwash yourself into believing that your paycheck works for you, not the other way around. They're literally paying for the privilege of having your time because you're the chosen one. Number three, get regular proof that you're competent in places where no one can rate you on a scale of one to five. Run half a marathon, train for HYROX, become a parent of any kind, pets, humans, or develop an unnecessarily strong opinion about coffee and tea. At the end of the day, it's not total freedom, but at least now you can convince yourself that you've transcended it. And as a bonus, you'll have hobbies to show for it because it's the coolest thing that you can afford on a corporate salary today. \n\nText shown on screen:\nIf your self-respect is OK after working corporate, you don't need this.",
